<!-- TECHNICAL -->
<div class="section" id="technical">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-icon">⚙️</div>
    <div class="section-title">Technical Questions & Answers</div>
  </div>
 
  <div class="question">"Walk me through how your team deployed OpenShift 4 across C2S and SC2S — start to finish."</div>
  <div class="answer">"We started in the AWS cost calculator to size the environment — CPU, memory, storage — based on workload requirements for that classification tier. Once approved, we provisioned EC2 instances in either C2S for Secret workloads or SC2S for TS/SCI. From there we stood up the OpenShift 4 cluster on those instances — I had sudo and root access throughout, so I was doing the actual platform installation, not just observing. Once the cluster was up, we ran Ansible playbooks for STIG hardening and security baseline enforcement across the RHEL and CoreOS nodes. Prisma Cloud went in for runtime container security monitoring, Keycloak for SSO and RBAC across projects, Splunk universal forwarders on every node shipping to the indexer, and GitLab for source control and pipeline management. Confluence handled our documentation. Full cycle — cost calculator to production-ready classified environment — we were completing that in about two days once the process was dialed in."</div>
 
  <div class="question">"What was different between C2S and SC2S?"</div>
  <div class="answer">"The classification boundary was the fundamental difference. C2S is AWS at the Secret level — SC2S is TS/SCI, more restricted with tighter access controls and a smaller footprint of approved services. The deployment process was largely the same — same Terraform IaC, same Ansible playbooks — but the network segmentation between tiers was a hard boundary. No data moved between C2S and SC2S without an approved transfer process. Within each environment, OpenShift network policies controlled pod-to-pod communication. Security posture requirements were stricter in SC2S — more controls to validate, tighter audit logging requirements, which is where the Splunk forwarder configuration was especially important."</div>
 
  <div class="question">"How did you structure Terraform in a classified environment?"</div>
  <div class="answer">"Modular structure — separate modules for networking, compute, and platform components so we could reuse them across environments without duplicating code. State management was kept within the classified environment itself — no remote state outside the boundary. We maintained separate state backends per classification tier so C2S state was completely isolated from SC2S. Terraform workspaces helped manage dev, test, and prod within each tier without state collision."</div>
 
  <div class="question">"Walk me through the Ansible playbooks during hardening."</div>
  <div class="answer">"The playbooks covered the full STIG hardening baseline for RHEL and CoreOS nodes — package management, service hardening, file permission enforcement, audit logging configuration, and security baseline settings aligned to DISA STIGs. Structured as roles for modularity so we could run specific roles independently when remediating a finding without touching the whole baseline. Idempotency was critical — we ran those playbooks multiple times during a deployment cycle and needed consistent outcomes every run. We also used Ansible to configure Splunk forwarder deployment and Keycloak integration settings post-cluster standup."</div>
 
  <div class="question">"Tell me about the Splunk implementation — forwarders, indexing, configuration."</div>
  <div class="answer">"We deployed Splunk universal forwarders on every OpenShift node — control plane and workers — shipping logs to a centralized Splunk indexer within the classified environment. The forwarders were configured to capture OS-level audit logs, OpenShift platform logs, and application-level events. On the indexer side we built correlation searches and dashboards to surface indicators of anomalous activity — failed authentication, privilege escalation events, unusual network connections between pods. The forwarder deployment itself was automated through Ansible as part of our standard hardening playbook, so every new node that came up was automatically configured and shipping logs within the deployment cycle."</div>
 
  <div class="question">"How is AWS troubleshooting different on EC2 vs physical hardware?"</div>
  <div class="answer">"The core Linux diagnostic commands are identical — top, ps aux, free -h, vmstat, iostat, df -h, journalctl. The additional layer on EC2 is checking CloudWatch metrics before SSH'ing in if you have console access, verifying you're not hitting EBS IOPS or throughput limits rather than a physical disk constraint, and on burstable T-series instances checking CPU credit balance since a depleted credit pool looks like a CPU bottleneck but the fix is completely different. In ADC air-gapped environments the CloudWatch access path may vary, so knowing the OS-level commands cold is essential regardless."</div>
</div>
 
