  <div class="question">"Tell me about a time something broke during a deployment and how you handled it."</div>
  <div class="answer">"During one of our SC2S deployments, we had a Splunk forwarder configuration that conflicted with an existing SELinux policy on the node — causing the forwarder to fail silently, so logs weren't shipping and we didn't know immediately. I caught it during post-deployment validation — the indexer wasn't receiving data from two nodes. I SSH'd in, reviewed the logs, isolated the conflict to a specific SELinux policy blocking the forwarder's socket. Remediated the playbook to account for that policy, re-ran the role on the affected nodes, validated the fix, and updated the playbook in GitLab with a comment documenting exactly what the conflict was and why the fix worked. After that we added a forwarder connectivity validation step to our deployment checklist so we'd catch it immediately on future deployments."</div>
 
  <div class="question">"How do you balance security requirements with delivery timelines?"</div>
  <div class="answer">"By embedding security into the process rather than treating it as a separate gate. At DIA we couldn't deploy fast and then spend a week hardening — the timeline didn't allow it and the classification environment didn't either. So we built security into the automation from day one. STIG hardening ran with every deployment. Prisma Cloud was configured before we handed an environment over. Splunk forwarders were part of the same Ansible run as the platform standup. Security and delivery weren't in tension — they happened simultaneously. The two-day deployment cycle was only possible because we didn't treat security as an afterthought."</div>
 
  <div class="question">"Tell me about working with a government customer in a classified program."</div>
